parts.I:
MISTAKES: d/dx e^x = x e^(x-1) (WRONG: power rule misuse);
   d/dx 2^x = x 2^(x-1) (WRONG; correct 2^x ln 2);
   d/dx ln(x^2) = 1/x^2 (WRONG; correct 2/x). Degrees instead of
   radians in trig derivatives.

parts.C:
SYMBOLS: y the function; f(x) > 0 the base and g(x) the exponent;
   y'/y = (ln y)' is the logarithmic derivative (relative rate of
   change).

parts.E:
CONDITIONS: f continuous on [a, b] (or at least bounded with finitely
   many jumps) guarantees the integral exists. It is a NUMBER (unlike the
   indefinite integral, a family of functions).
   Properties:
     int[a..a] f = 0;  int[b..a] f = - int[a..b] f;
     int[a..b] (c f + g) = c int f + int g;
     int[a..b] f = int[a..c] f + int[c..b] f;
     if f >= g on [a,b] then int f >= int g;
     |int[a..b] f| <= int[a..b] |f|.

parts.I:
MISTAKE: "the terms get closer to each other, so it converges" is true
   for sequences (Cauchy) but the analogous idea for series is wrong:
   terms -> 0 does not make a series converge.

MISTAKES: using the series outside its interval of convergence
   (e.g. ln(1+x) series at x = 3); forgetting n! in the denominator;
   forgetting (x - a) when a != 0.

parts.E:
CONDITIONS: only for square matrices. Properties:
     det(AB) = det(A) det(B);  det(A^T) = det(A);
     det(A^(-1)) = 1/det(A);   det(cA) = c^n det(A)  (n x n matrix; NOT
     c det A);
     swapping two rows flips the sign; multiplying a row by c multiplies
     det by c; adding a multiple of one row to another changes nothing;
     a zero row, or two equal/proportional rows => det = 0;
     det(A + B) != det(A) + det(B).
     A is invertible <=> det(A) != 0.
     det(A) = product of eigenvalues (Module 9).

parts.I:
MISTAKES: arithmetic slips (re-check by substituting); multiplying a
   row by 0; operating on only the coefficient side and not the RHS;
   dividing by a (near-)zero pivot without swapping rows.

